meta {
  name: Reactivate User
  type: http
  seq: 11
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/users/{{activeUserId}}/reactivate
  auth: bearer
  body: none
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("the user is active again", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.isActive).to.equal(true);
    expect(body.deactivatedAt).to.equal(null);
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /users/:userId/reactivate

  Restore a removed employee's access. They can sign in again and their
  permissions resolve as before.

  **Module** — none (`@SkipModule`). **Permission** — `agency:users:write`.

  **Response `201`** — the full user detail, same shape as `GET /users/:userId`.

  **Errors**
  - `403` — caller lacks `agency:users:write`.
  - `404` — no such user in this agency, or the target is a platform admin.
  - `409` — that user has not been removed.

  ## What this does *not* do
  It does not restore the work released when they were removed. Those tickets sat
  in the unassigned queue and may well have been picked up by someone else;
  silently yanking them back would be worse than making reassignment explicit.

  ## Why it runs last
  It is this pair's **cleanup** — **Deactivate User** removes the demo producer
  and this puts them back, so a repeated `npx @usebruno/cli run` leaves the
  tenant's logins intact. Note the caveat in **Deactivate User**: their released
  tickets stay unassigned, which only a re-seed restores.
}
